WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.042
YURI GRINSHTEYN: During
an incident or an outage,

00:00:02.042 --> 00:00:04.230
your first priority is
triage and mitigation, not

00:00:04.230 --> 00:00:05.605
root cause analysis.

00:00:05.605 --> 00:00:07.980
But there may be times you
still need to debug especially

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.063
in production if that's
the only environment where

00:00:10.063 --> 00:00:11.520
you can reproduce the issue.

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:13.830
But you can't afford to
redeploy to add more logging

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:14.810
or debugging code.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.050
So what do you do?

00:00:16.050 --> 00:00:18.790
Today, we'll look at debugging
in production, the Stackdriver

00:00:18.790 --> 00:00:19.500
Debugger.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:20.940
This is the Stack doctor.

00:00:27.970 --> 00:00:30.220
In the past, situations like
these required developers

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:32.320
to add additional
instrumentation to their code

00:00:32.320 --> 00:00:34.595
and wait for a new deployment
to get more insight.

00:00:34.595 --> 00:00:36.970
But what if you could just
inspect the code in production

00:00:36.970 --> 00:00:39.760
or add logging on the fly
without having to redeploy?

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:42.220
This is what Stackdriver
Debugger is built for.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:43.880
Let's look at an
example node.js app

00:00:43.880 --> 00:00:45.970
to see how to add
debugger to your service.

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.520
We'll put the link to the
repo in the video description.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.835
We add the debugger agent
at the very top of our code

00:00:50.835 --> 00:00:52.210
and give the agent
the parameters

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:54.070
needed to authenticate
to the API

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:56.470
and specify the service
we'll be debugging.

00:00:56.470 --> 00:00:58.128
Once our app is
running, we can go

00:00:58.128 --> 00:00:59.920
to the debugger screen
in the cloud console

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:01.545
and see that our
service has checked in

00:01:01.545 --> 00:01:03.040
and is ready for debugging.

00:01:03.040 --> 00:01:04.629
The next step is
to tell Debugger

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:05.980
where our source code is.

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:07.540
Our sample app's
code is on GitHub,

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:08.963
so we can select that option.

00:01:08.963 --> 00:01:10.630
But we can have
Debugger use source code

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:13.450
from Google Cloud Source
Repositories, GitLab,

00:01:13.450 --> 00:01:16.332
or Bitbucket, or even from
our local workstation.

00:01:16.332 --> 00:01:18.290
Once we select the location
of the source code,

00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:19.370
it's shown on the screen.

00:01:19.370 --> 00:01:22.300
Our setup is done, and we
can start using Debugger.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.770
Debugger lets us debug in
production in two ways.

00:01:24.770 --> 00:01:26.843
First, it allows us
to create snapshots

00:01:26.843 --> 00:01:28.760
that let us inspect the
state of our variables

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:30.620
that specify points in the code.

00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.130
The second, lets us have
more logging on the fly.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:34.690
Let's take a look at both.

00:01:34.690 --> 00:01:36.880
To create a new snapshot,
click on a line of code

00:01:36.880 --> 00:01:39.408
where you would like to capture
the state of our variables.

00:01:39.408 --> 00:01:41.200
The next time the line
of code is executed,

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.760
a snapshot is created.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:45.220
A snapshot shows us the
values of the local variables

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.320
at the specified
execution point.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.000
We can also add
expressions to capture

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.740
static or global
variables that are not

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:53.410
part of the local variable
set or perform calculations

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:55.090
like a duration.

00:01:55.090 --> 00:01:57.070
We've now seen how to
use snapshots to inspect

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:58.455
variable values in our code.

00:01:58.455 --> 00:01:59.830
This is really
helpful when we're

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:02.570
debugging to figure out if
there's a mistake in our code.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:05.290
But what if we want to
add more debug logging?

00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:07.926
Stackdriver Debugger also allows
us to add logging on the fly.

00:02:07.926 --> 00:02:09.759
That is to essentially
create additional log

00:02:09.759 --> 00:02:12.220
entries that get ingested
into Stackdriver Logging.

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:14.970
Adding one is as easy as
switching to the logpoint tab,

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.990
selecting a line of
code, and then specifying

00:02:16.990 --> 00:02:18.242
the message to be written.

00:02:18.242 --> 00:02:20.200
You can use this to log
the value of a variable

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:22.617
and then use the Log Viewer
to see the value for each time

00:02:22.617 --> 00:02:24.190
the line of code is hit.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:26.470
Once we add the logpoint,
additional log entries

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:27.490
are created.

00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:29.668
The logpoint will
persist for 24 hours.

00:02:29.668 --> 00:02:31.960
This is a great way to get
better debugging information

00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.350
to more clearly understand what
our code is actually doing.

00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:37.210
Debugging in production
sounds scary,

00:02:37.210 --> 00:02:38.860
but Stackdriver
Debugger makes it easy

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.068
and lets you do it without
having to change your code

00:02:41.068 --> 00:02:42.550
or redeploy your app.

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.050
Thank you for joining me today,
and come back soon for more.

00:02:45.050 --> 00:02:46.180
This is the Stack Doctor.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.390
Stay healthy out there.

